The problems associated with defining porosity in molecular crystals were recently highlighted7. For example, ‘virtual porosity’ is sometimes created by deleting guests or counterions from a crystal structure, or by representing the structure in a potentially misleading way — such as ball-and-stick plots rather than space-filling models7. For the purposes of this Perspective, we define porosity as the ability to adsorb guests, irrespective of whether formally interconnected pore channels exist. This definition takes into account the phenomenon of porosity ‘without pores’7, where molecular solids with disconnected void volume nonetheless adsorb considerable quantities of guests8 by cooperative diffusion mechanisms9. We focus here on porous organic molecules, rather than metal–organic species10, with particular emphasis on recently published systems (2006–2010). We have also limited discussion to organic molecules that can be prepared by stepwise organic synthesis, rather than materials, such as single-walled carbon nanotubes, that can also demonstrate permanent porosity11.
## Porous molecules versus porous networks
Porous networks such as polymers and MOFs can show a range of functionalities, and have now attained Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface areas $(S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}})$ of more than $5{,}500\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (refs 12, 13). Given the challenge in creating robust porosity in molecular materials without extended directional covalent or coordination bonding, what then are the potential benefits of porous molecules in comparison with porous networks? Some distinguishing features of porous molecules are listed below.
• Solubility. Unlike porous networks, discrete molecules may be dissolved in water or common organic solvents. This can give processing advantages — for example, in the solution casting of thin porous films14 or in structure modification by recrystallization15.   
• Mix-and-match strategies. Molecular solubility also indicates the potential to mix different functionalities within a single solid — for example, by cocrystallization of more than one molecular ‘pore’. This could be used to produce porous materials containing incompatible chemical entities — for example, site-isolated acid and base groups incorporated in separate molecular cages, thus mimicking the spatial isolation of functionality attained in biological structures such as enzymes. Synthetic diversification. Functional group modification and subsequent purification of discrete ‘porous’ molecules may, in some cases, be more readily achieved in solution than equivalent heterogeneous chemical transformations in extended networks.   
• Mobility. Molecular crystals — and amorphous molecular solids — are not interconnected by strong covalent or coordination bonds. As such, porous organic molecules have potential to respond to guest sorption in ways that might be prohibited, for example, in even the most flexible $\mathrm{MOF}s^{1,2}$ . This could in turn allow the cooperative trapping of specific guests — for example, in applications such as carbon capture, molecular detection and removal of atmospheric pollutants.
## intrinsic and extrinsic porosity
It is possible to classify porous molecules as either intrinsically or extrinsically porous (Fig. 1). We define intrinsic porosity as that which results from the structure of the discrete molecule as viewed in isolation. This applies to bowl-shaped molecules, such as calixarenes8, as well as ‘porous organic cages’15, which possess prefabricated cavities and pore windows. By contrast, extrinsically porous molecules are those that pack together to form porous structures in the solid state, but where the porosity is not intrinsic to the isolated molecular geometry. There are of course limitations to these definitions: for example, the linear polymer PIM- $\cdot1^{14}$ has intrinsic ‘porosity’, which could be identified in a molecular model of a single random coil, as well as intermolecular pore volume, which arises from inefficient packing of the contorted chains in the solid state. Moreover, as we shall see, there are examples of ‘intrinsically’ porous molecules that show extrinsic porosity16. These definitions are helpful, however, in describing porous systems, and they highlight two complementary and non-exclusive strategies for introducing porosity into molecular solids. We also note that the concept of ‘intrinsic’ and ‘extrinsic’ porosity has been invoked for hypothetical microporous liquids17.
## crystalline porous molecules
Crystalline porous molecules are desirable because their long-range molecular order offers potential for fine structural control in applications such as molecular separations. A major challenge, however, is that molecular crystals containing cavities or channels tend to be isolated from solution as solvate phases. These solvates do not commonly retain their incipient porosity on guest removal, but rather collapse to form a denser phase that may be either crystalline or amorphous. As such, molecular crystals that retain permanent micropore structures on desolvation should be considered atypical.
Extrinsically porous organic crystals. A number of organic molecules that pack inefficiently in the solid state and are stable to desolvation, thus forming permanently porous molecular crystals, have been reported. An early example is Dianin’s compound, 4-hydroxyphenyl-2,3,4-trimethylchroman18. This relatively simple molecule features a bent structure that packs inefficiently to form hour-glassshaped one-dimensional channels within the crystals19. The material adsorbs a number of different molecular guests18.
Another extrinsically porous molecule is tris-o-phenylenedioxycyclotriphosphazene (TPP), which features a phosphazene $\left(\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\right)$ core with three orthogonal catechol rings. TPP crystallizes to form a structure with one-dimensional hexagonal-shaped pores that are accessible to gases such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (ref. 20).
Porosity is also found in certain crystalline dipeptides including l-alanyl- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotL}$ -valine, l-valyl-l-alanine, l-isoleucyl-l-valine and l-valyl-l-isoleucine. A one-dimensional channel motif in dipeptides has been known for some time21 and probed by both crystallography and $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}\ \mathrm{NMR}^{22}$ . More recently, the adsorption of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has been studied in dipeptide crystals23. For example, it was demonstrated that l-isoleucyl-l-valine could show efficient selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH_{4}}$ at pressures of less than 0.07 bar. All of these materials are extrinsically porous and it would be difficult to infer the porous properties of the crystals by simple examination of the isolated molecular structures.
A useful approach for discovering porous organic molecules is to search the Cambridge Structural Database, or other crystallographic databases, for candidate structures — for example, by looking for materials with anomalously low crystallographic densities. This data mining strategy proved successful recently in uncovering the porous nature of the crystalline material $_{3,3^{\prime}-4,4^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis (trimethylsilylethynyl)biphenyl (4TMSEBP) (Fig. 1a)24. Crystalline 4TMSEBP was found to be stable to desolvation, and the desolvated material showed internal voids with diameters of $11\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , generated by the orthogonal intersection of $\mathrm{~\ddot{4}~\AA~}$ channels in three dimensions. The pore structure was compared to a Schwarz P triply periodic minimal surface — a mathematically derived topology where the mean curvature of the repeating surface is equal to zero.
In general, the ab initio prediction of appropriate molecular tectons25–27 for extrinsically porous organic molecules is very challenging, although recent advances in crystal-structure prediction28 suggest more scope for this in the future. This may allow predictive approaches that are similar to those applied successfully for $\operatorname{MOFs}^{29}$ .
Calixarenes and macrocycles. Macrocycles are good candidates for intrinsically porous molecules, as they typically possess a prefabricated ‘hole’ of some type. Calix $[n]$ arenes are a family of oligomeric macrocycles where $n=4{-}9$ repeat units. In particular, four-membered calix[4]arenes are molecules with a permanent cone-shaped molecular cavity, and are an important cornerstone for this research area30. The well-known studies by Atwood and colleagues focused initially on formally nonporous crystalline materials, which nonetheless adsorb large volumes of various guests by cooperative diffusion mechanisms8,9,30. More recent reports have also demonstrated permanent, conventional microporosity in calixarene crystals. For example, the crystal structure for 1,2-dimethoxy- $\boldsymbol{p}$ -tert-butylcalix[4]-dihydroquinone31,32 features channel-linked cavities and large unoccupied lattice voids. Structural integrity was retained on water removal, as confirmed by powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction, and a $S A_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $230{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ was measured by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption at $77\mathrm{K}$ .
Other macrocycles illustrate the distinction between intrinsic and extrinsic porosity. For example, a bis-urea macrocycle was shown to form a porous crystalline apohost with one-dimensional channels33, assembled by intermolecular urea–urea hydrogen-bonding interactions and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking. The $S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}$ for the desolvated material was found to be $316~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , as calculated from the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $0{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The one-dimensional pore channel runs through the macrocycle — that is, the porosity may be defined as intrinsic. The same team also reported recently a diacetylene macrocycle that shows stability towards desolvation and a $S A_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $350~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ calculated from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption34. These two intrinsically porous systems may be contrasted with cucurbit[6]uril (CB[6]), which is a tubular six-membered macrocycle comprising methylene-bridged glycouril units. The hydrophobic cavity in CB[6] has a diameter of $5.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and is accessible through two polar carbonyl-lined portals with a diameter of $4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ . The synthesis of CB[6] using HCl led to crystallization into a new polymorph featuring the hexagonal arrangement of one-dimensional channels filled with disordered $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules16. The CB[6] cavities were found to trap water guests and to be inaccessible to further guest uptake in this polymorph. As such, in contrast to the bis-urea example described above33, this is an example of an ‘intrinsically porous’ molecule crystallizing in an extrinsically porous form with a $S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}$ of $210~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Porous organic cages. Crystalline organic hosts with enforced covalent cavities, such as cavitands35, cryptophanes36, carcerands37 and self-assembled capsules38, have been known for many years, and these molecules and assemblies often resemble prefabricated covalent organic ‘pores’. Surprisingly, however, reports of permanent microporosity in such systems are uncommon, either because the relevant measurements have not been made or because the materials crystallize as solvates that lose their long-range molecular order on desolvation. We recently described a series of tetrahedral imine-linked cage molecules synthesized by the $\left[4+6\right]$ condensation of 1,3,5-triformylbenzene with three different vicinal diamines: 1,2-ethylenediamine (cage 1), 1,2-propylenediamine (cage 2) and $(R,R)$ -1,2-diaminocyclohexane (cage 3)15. The structure of the tetrahedral cage molecule produced from the reaction of 1,3,5-triformylbenzene with $(R,R)$ -1,2-diaminocyclohexane (cage 3) is shown in Fig. 1b. Each cage has four windows with diameters in the range of $5.8\substack{-6.1\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ : that is, large enough to admit small organic molecules and gases.
The nature of the resulting porosity depends on the degree of alignment of the cage windows between molecules, which is in turn determined by the cage vertex functionality15. Cage 3 is mainly intrinsically porous in nature, and crystallizes in the solid state to form a material with a $S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}$ of $624{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , the highest yet reported for a purely organic molecular crystal. This crystalline material is also a homochiral microporous solid, perhaps suggesting applications in chiral molecular separations. These cages also illustrate a potential advantage of molecular solubility. For example, it was found that the nonporous cage 1 could be recrystallized from a mixture of dichloromethane and $\textit{o}$ -xylene to produce a different, permanently porous polymorph $(S A_{\mathrm{BET}}=550\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . This suggests new materials may be possible in the future, such as organic solids where porosity can be switched ‘on’ or ‘off ’ in response to an external stimulus. Although these molecules have ultramicropores $(<1\ \mathrm{nm})$ 1 and are therefore restricted to small guests, the design of porous analogues to accept larger guests is conceivable — for example, to capture larger organic pollutants — by drawing on the diverse ‘cage’ chemistry that has already been developed35–39. The recent development of mechanically interlocked cage catenanes with permanent cavities presents another strategy, although these materials as yet do not show permanent porosity40.
Phthalocyanine nanoporous crystals. Although not purely organic in nature, the recent report of nanoporous metal phthalocyanine crystals41 with high surface areas $(S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}=850–1,000~\mathrm{{m}^{2}~g^{-1})}$ illustrates a new strategy for stabilizing porous crystals towards desolvation, which could find wholly organic analogues in the future. Phthalocyanines are a family of intensely coloured planar macrocycles that feature four pyrrole-based subunits linked by nitrogen atoms to form a 16-membered ring (Fig. 2, left). A recent study showed that various substituted metal phthalocyanine complexes form molecular crystals with solvent-filled voids and incipient microporosity41. These solvates, however, lose their long-range molecular order during desolvation. To address this, the axial ligands attached to the phthalocyanine-bound metal were replaced through exchange reactions and this provided a strategy for stabilizing the molecular crystals. In particular, ditopic bipyridyl ligands were exchanged into the crystals, thus bridging adjacent hexa-phthalocyanine assemblies in a manner that is analogous to the use of wall ties to stabilize cavity brick walls (Fig. 2)41. These bridged materials could be desolvated to form highly microporous materials that have parallels with MOFs, but which are molecular crystals rather than extended coordination networks. The findings suggest a more general design strategy for stabilizing porous molecular crystals. For example, single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformations might be envisaged in wholly organic materials, which involve dynamic covalent-exchange reactions40,42 rather than metal-coordination chemistry.
## amorphous porous molecules
An alternative strategy for producing porous materials is to design molecules that cannot pack effectively in the solid state, irrespective of molecular orientation or processing conditions. In principle, this provides a number of advantages over porous molecular crystals and might overcome the problem of porosity loss in crystals on desolvation.
Polymers of intrinsic microporosity. Polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs) have been reviewed recently43 and are treated only briefly here. These soluble polymers have rigid, contorted molecular structures that are unable to pack efficiently in the solid state. A unique advantage of linear PIMs is the ability to cast microporous films with good mechanical properties (Fig. 3a)14,44. This gives rise to potential applications in gas separation where thin films and high gas flux are desirable. Most PIMs have been synthesized through a highly efficient dibenzodioxane-forming reaction using biscatechol-type monomers14, though soluble spirobifluorene-linked polyamides and polyimides have also been prepared45. Soluble linear PIMs can show a $S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}$ as high as $895\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (ref. 46).
Amorphous porous macrocycles. One difference between polymers and smaller organic molecules is the increased reorganizational energy barrier that results from entanglements between polymer chains. Hence, permanent porosity in polymers with high free volume is, at least in part, a function of their macromolecular chain structure.
For example, the level of porosity in PIM-1 increases with increasing molecular weight14. It is unsurprising, then, that high levels of permanent porosity in amorphous phases of small molecules is extremely rare. It has been shown recently47 that a cyclic waterwheel-like oligomer, Noria48, shows gas adsorption properties in the amorphous state. Noria has a large internal cavity with a volume of $160\textup{\AA}^{3}$ and a portal diameter of approximately $\dot{5}\dot{\mathrm{~A~}}$ (Fig. 3b). The material was isolated in a completely amorphous form, as determined by powder X-ray diffraction and scanning electron microscopy (Fig. 3b). A $S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}$ of $40~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was measured by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . However, when $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was used as the adsorbate, then surface areas of $250{-}280\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ were reported. Calculations based on the internal cavity volume for the Noria molecule indicated that only a fraction of the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ could be accommodated, implying the presence of both intrinsic and extrinsic porosity in the sample. By attaching bulky tert-butyloxycarbonyl or methacrylate groups to the Noria structure, the internal volume could be increased to approximately $350\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ .
undiscovered porous organic molecules?
It is perhaps useful to consider molecules in the literature that have not yet been identified as porous, but which might show porosity on further examination. Low crystallographic density has already been highlighted as one possible search criterion24, but this is only possible for crystalline materials where X-ray structures have been reported. Intrinsic molecular porosity is also a helpful guide, but this may or may not be realised as accessible porosity in the solid state. Taking a cue from our own work15, a range of cage-type molecules exist that may have future use as porous organic materials. A detailed discussion is not possible here owing to the large number of potential systems35–39, but Warmuth and co-workers, for example, have reported organic cages with impressively large internal cavity volumes as determined from molecular models49. Similarly, Murakami50, Mastalerz51 and Chen52 have described rigid organic cages that feature a non-collapsible cavity in the centre. Crystal structures were not reported for these materials, but one can imagine the potential for porous amorphous or crystalline phases by analogy with Noria47 and porous imine cages15, respectively (Fig. 4).
## summary and outlook
Porous organic molecules display some striking differences in comparison with porous networks, both in terms of processability and physical properties. There is much scope to discover new porous molecules for applications such as gas separation, energy storage and catalysis. As yet, however, surface areas in porous molecules $(S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}=600-1,000\ \mathrm{{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{{g}^{-1})^{15,41}}}$ do not approach those attained in networks. Indeed, comparison with the state-of-the-art for networks $(S A_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{BET}}=5,500{-}6,200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})^{12,13}$ would suggest that more radical design strategies are required. For example, to achieve such high surface areas may require a molecular solid with a remarkably low density in the range, say, of $0.1{-}0.4\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .
A difficult challenge here will be to avoid the ‘double-wall problem’ in the crystalline (and amorphous) packing of both extrinsically and intrinsically porous molecules. This arises from the fact that molecular solids are held together by van der Waals packing forces, typically expressed in close intermolecular contacts such as $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking between aromatic rings. In essence, two $\pi$ -stacked arene rings generate around half of the specific molecular surface that is produced by a biphenyl-linker in a covalent molecular network12. Ideally, one would seek porous molecular solids with ‘point contacts’ between molecules or, at least, intermolecular contacts which consume as little molecular surface as possible. As such, it may prove that directional non-covalent forces such as hydrogen- or halogen-bonding are required27, perhaps in addition to intrinsic molecular porosity, to produce molecules with much higher surface areas.
Raw surface area, however, is not everything. All new porous materials are ultimately competing with commercial products, such as activated carbons, which already show high surface areas $(S A_{\mathrm{{BET}}}$ $>3,000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ), and are scalable, chemically and thermally stable, and comparatively inexpensive. Researchers should therefore contemplate the design of porous molecules that show ‘chemical added value’ that cannot readily be obtained in networks. The positional and conformational flexibility of discrete molecules — which renders permanent porosity a challenge — might present practical opportunities here. For example, we envisage porous organic molecules that can rapidly adsorb low-level organic pollutants from the atmosphere and where adsorption triggers a structural transformation to a non-porous form, thus trapping the pollutant.
We also foresee a modular strategy for the construction of porous solids where individual organic pores are engineered — perhaps with quite complex multi-step organic transformations — before combining them in a porous molecular cocrystal (or ‘co-glass’47). This might allow the production of complex porous molecular machines with combined functionality that would be difficult to attain in a one-step solvothermal network synthesis or in a network post-modification strategy. Porous polymer molecules might also be blended with intrinsically porous small molecules to generate materials with enhanced functions — for example, the mechanical strength of a $\mathrm{PIM}^{14,43-46}$ combined with a chemically functionalized porous organic cage15,40. This research area therefore suggests future practical opportunities as well as presenting a fascinating set of fundamental challenges for synthetic chemists, materials scientists, crystal engineers and crystallographers.
